Proposed verification criteria:
1. Given the Contentstack client is mocked to return a draft entry containing unpublished changes (e.g., a modified page title and body), when a content author triggers live preview from CMS, the rendered page displays the unpublished title and body from that draft entry.
2. When no preview signal is present in the page request, the rendered page displays the published version of the content exactly as it currently does — existing page rendering is unchanged.
3. Given the Contentstack client is mocked, when a live preview is triggered for a page entry, the request our application sends to Contentstack includes the live_preview hash, content_type_uid, and entry_uid parameters.
4. Given the Contentstack client is mocked to return a draft entry for a GO-branded page, the rendered page displays the draft content with GO branding and styling intact; the same holds for UP-branded and MX-branded pages.
5. When live preview is attempted on a protected page, the page does not crash or display an unhandled error — the user is informed that preview is unavailable for that page, or the page renders gracefully within the stated limitation.
6. Given the Contentstack client is mocked to return a draft entry, when the content author navigates between pages during a live preview session, the preview state persists across page navigations within that session until the preview signal is no longer present.

Apply these rules EXACTLY (the producer is held to the same text — do not invent stricter or looser criteria):
A verification criterion states WHAT AN END USER OR TESTER OBSERVES on the user-facing surface THE TICKET IS ABOUT — the rendered output for an output ticket, the displayed screen for a UI ticket, the response a CLIENT receives for an API ticket. It is a BLACK-BOX check on that surface. It NEVER describes HOW the value is produced.
A verification criterion is FORBIDDEN if it:
- prescribes HOW to implement — any algorithm, mechanism, approach, or the addition/reading of any new field, flag or service;
- references an INTERNAL structure that merely FEEDS the ticket's surface — an intermediate payload, a data-transfer object, or a specific response field used to BUILD the output. Verify the surface the ticket names, NOT the data structure behind it;
- makes a CROSS-COMPARISON that presumes a mechanism — never assert one value "must equal" / "matches" / "is the same as" another; that presumes a shared derivation. Assert the required value is present and correct ON ITS OWN.
Every verification criterion must be observable, testable, and tied to the ticket's stated symptom/intent.

FLAG any verification criterion that violates ANY rule above, OR that fails to cover the intent of an acceptance criterion.
Output ONLY a JSON array of short flag strings, e.g. ["VC 2 prescribes halving — restate as observable outcome"]. Output [] if every VC is clean. No prose, no markdown.

